  "sheetname": "GermanPronouns3Du",
  "title": "du — You (s)",
  "description": "Practise changing the object with du: present, future and past.",
  "updatedDate": 20261015,
  "intro": "Every example uses Du as its subject, meaning you (s) in this file. Dich/dir refer back to the subject in the self-reference rows. Seeing oneself can describe a mirror or photo. Keep the subject fixed and vary whom the subject sees or to whom the subject gives the book. Sehen takes an accusative object. Geben takes a dative recipient plus das Buch as the accusative thing. The highlighted key is the object or recipient. English labels: (s) singular, (p) plural, (f) formal.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "du — changing the object",
      "sortorder": 1,
      "explanation": "Start with the present-tense accusative and dative lists, then compare the same object choices in Futur I and Perfekt. Short sentences omit final full stops.",
      "rule": "Du siehst + Akkusativ | Du gibst + Dativ + das Buch",
      "entries": [
        {
          "header": "sehen — Präsens — present",
          "kind": "pattern",
          "explanation": "The subject and verb stay fixed. Change the accusative object.",
          "pattern": "Du siehst + Akkusativ",
          "examples": [
            {
              "german": "Du siehst mich",
              "english": "You (s) see me",
              "focus": "mich",
              "note": "",
              "key": "mich"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst dich",
              "english": "You (s) see yourself",
              "focus": "dich",
              "note": "Reflexive reading: yourself.",
              "key": "dich"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst ihn",
              "english": "You (s) see him",
              "focus": "ihn",
              "note": "",
              "key": "ihn"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst sie",
              "english": "You (s) see her",
              "focus": "sie",
              "note": "Singular feminine: her.",
              "key": "sie"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst es",
              "english": "You (s) see it",
              "focus": "es",
              "note": "",
              "key": "es"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst uns",
              "english": "You (s) see us",
              "focus": "uns",
              "note": "",
              "key": "uns"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst euch",
              "english": "You (s) see you (p)",
              "focus": "euch",
              "note": "",
              "key": "euch"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst sie",
              "english": "You (s) see them",
              "focus": "sie",
              "note": "Plural: them. Same German spelling as her; context distinguishes the meaning.",
              "key": "sie"
            },
            {
              "german": "Du siehst Sie",
              "english": "You (s) see you (f)",
              "focus": "Sie",
              "note": "Formal object or recipient: always capitalized; one or more addressees.",
              "key": "Sie"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "header": "geben — Präsens — present",
          "kind": "pattern",
          "explanation": "The subject, verb and das Buch stay fixed. Change only the dative recipient. Ihm is translated as him here, but can also refer to a neuter noun.",
          "pattern": "Du gibst + Dativ + das Buch",
          "examples": [
            {
              "german": "Du gibst mir das Buch",
